> This handbook is the definitive guide for all Customer Support team members at NexaTech Solutions. It outlines our support philosophy, procedures, and the standards of excellence we are committed to delivering to our customers.

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1. Our Support Philosophy

At NexaTech, we view customer support not as a cost center, but as a crucial driver of customer loyalty, retention, and growth. Our philosophy is centered on three key principles: **Empathy**, **Expertise**, and **Efficiency**. We strive to understand our customers' challenges, provide expert guidance to resolve their issues, and do so in a timely and efficient manner. Every support interaction is an opportunity to strengthen our relationship with the customer and reinforce the value of our partnership.

### 1.2. Purpose of this Handbook

This handbook provides a comprehensive framework for delivering consistent, high-quality support. It details our support channels, service level agreements (SLAs), issue-handling procedures, and the tools we use to serve our customers. All members of the Customer Success and Support organization are expected to be familiar with and adhere to the guidelines within this document.

## 2. Support Channels

We offer multiple channels for customers to seek assistance, ensuring accessibility and convenience.

*   **Help Center & Knowledge Base:** A self-service portal with articles, tutorials, and FAQs. This is the first line of support.
*   **Ticketing System (Zendesk):** The primary channel for customers to submit support requests. All issues are tracked and managed through this system.
*   **Live Chat:** Available for real-time assistance with quick questions and minor issues.
*   **Phone Support:** Reserved for critical and high-severity issues, primarily for our Enterprise-tier customers.

## 3. Support Tiers and Service Level Agreements (SLAs)

Our support services are tiered to align with our customer segments and the criticality of their issues. SLAs define our commitment to response and resolution times.

### 3.1. Issue Severity Levels

| Severity | Definition | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **1 - Critical** | System-wide outage, critical functionality unavailable, data corruption. | Platform is down; users cannot log in. |
| **2 - High** | Major functionality is impaired, significant performance degradation. | A key feature is not working; reports are failing to generate. |
| **3 - Normal** | Minor feature is not working as expected, or a question about functionality. | A button is misplaced; inquiry about a feature. |
| **4 - Low** | Cosmetic issue, general question, or feature request. | Typo in the UI; question about future plans. |

### 3.2. Service Level Agreements (SLAs)

